ncy key; the hacker Jinx carried the codes; the ex-enforcer carried the door charges. Everyone carried fear. Fear is fuel."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Remembering', 'text': "By dawn the Patrol had stood down — human, after all, and shown the same thirty seconds. Director Vane was gone by noon, the Algorithm's servers dark. the Outer Rings belonged to its people again. It had only ever been on loan.\n\nIris Novak and the hacker Jinx were in the tower when the door blew. The ex-enforcer held the stairs long enough. Long enough for what? For the footage to loop. For the memory to root. For Director Vane's lie to die on every screen at once.\n\nWhat happened in the next hour, historians argue about. What Iris Novak remembers: silence, then a sound like a held breath released, then the streets filling with people who had remembered they were people."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'After the Static', 'text': "Rebuilding the Outer Rings was harder than freeing it. Iris Novak organized food lines with the same care once used for heists. the hacker Jinx taught kids to code on salvaged terminals. The medic ran a clinic. The runner ran messages of hope.\n\nThe footage from a frequency key became the Outer Rings's founding document: not a constitution, but a memory. This is what we were. This is what we can be. freedom is a habit — carved, at the hacker Jinx's insistence, over the tower door.\n\nThere were trials. Director Vane's people faced justice — real justice, not the Algorithm's kind, with evidence and mercy both. Some were guilty. Some were just afraid. Iris Novak learned the difference matters."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'The Habit of Freedom', 'text': "freedom is a habit — Iris Novak thinks it every time, like a prayer, like a promise. Memory is resistance. Freedom is a habit. Practice daily.\n\nSometimes Iris Novak walks to the tower and looks out over the Outer Rings, green in the cracks of the concrete, alive in the way of things that remember. a frequency key is gone. What it carried isn't.\n\nthe hacker Jinx runs the network now — the real one, the open one, the one that belongs to everyone. The safehouse is quieter. The neon is warmer. It turns out freedom has a sound, and it's laughter."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

